{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04302","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04302","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04302","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas F","familyName":"Burns","name":"Burns, Thomas F","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Guessing is Rewarded: Rethinking Language Model Evaluation with Distributional Uncertainty Scoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:50:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T15:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard language model evaluation assigns scores to single predicted answers, rewarding high-confidence responses regardless of how residual probability mass is distributed over alternative options. This creates a systematic pressure toward overconfident guessing: under accuracy-based schemes, a model maximises its expected score by always committing to an answer rather than abstaining, even when its uncertainty is high. While penalty-based approaches partially address this by raising the confidence threshold for strategic guessing, they still treat all sub-threshold responses identically, ignoring a fundamental distinction in how models can express uncertainty - for example between hedging toward incorrect answers versus hedging toward \"I don't know\" responses. This paper introduces a novel evaluation metric to solve this problem of not considering a model's entire probability distribution over answer choices. The metric naturally distinguishes between harmful overconfidence in wrong answers and uncertainty expressed through abstention, providing scores in an interpretable default range. Through theoretical analysis and illustrative examples, the metric is shown to offer a more nuanced and aligned evaluation paradigm that incentivises models to express genuine uncertainty rather than guessing. Adapting 12 existing evaluation benchmarks to the metric's variants and measuring performance on six language models shows that for half of the tested benchmarks scores are negative across all tested models, indicating significant tendencies towards hallucination."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 2 figures; accepted to NeurIPS 2026 (Evaluations and Datasets track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04302","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:28:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04100","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04100","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04100","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jizhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Diwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Da","name":"Da, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiamo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh","familyName":"Kästner","name":"Kästner, Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harold","familyName":"Soh","name":"Soh, Harold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TOPO-Bench: An Open-Source Topological Mapping Evaluation Framework with Quantifiable Perceptual Aliasing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:58:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:52:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-09T02:53:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T00:17:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-12T13:18:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:38:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Topological mapping offers a compact and robust representation for navigation, but progress in the field is hindered by the lack of standardized evaluation metrics, datasets, and protocols. Existing systems are assessed using different environments and criteria, preventing fair and reproducible comparisons. Moreover, a key challenge - perceptual aliasing - remains under-quantified, despite its strong influence on system performance. We address these gaps by (1) formalizing topological consistency as the fundamental property of topological maps and showing that localization accuracy provides an efficient and interpretable surrogate metric, and (2) proposing the first quantitative measure of dataset ambiguity to enable fair comparisons across environments. To support this protocol, we curate a diverse benchmark dataset with calibrated ambiguity levels, implement and release deep-learned baseline systems, and evaluate them alongside classical methods. Our experiments and analysis yield new insights into the limitations of current approaches under perceptual aliasing. All datasets, baselines, and evaluation tools are fully open-sourced to foster consistent and reproducible research in topological mapping."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICRA 2026. Updated author list to match the conference version; added a reference and publication note. Jiaming Wang, Diwen Liu, and Jizhuo Chen contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04100","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:41:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi Evie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi Evie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freda","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Freda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autonomy Reshapes How Personalization Affects Privacy Concerns and Trust in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T12:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents require personal information for personalization in order to effectively act on users' behalf, but this raises privacy concerns that can discourage data sharing, limiting both the autonomy levels at which agents can operate and the effectiveness of personalization. Yet the expanded design space of agent autonomy also presents opportunities to shape these effects, which remain underexplored. We conducted a $3\\times3$ between-subjects experiment ($N=450$) to study how agent autonomy level influences personalization's effects on users' privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use, as well as the underlying psychological processes. We find that risk-contingent autonomy, where the agent delegates control back to users upon detecting potential privacy leakage, improves users' perceived control. This in turn attenuates personalization's adverse effects: privacy concerns rise less and trust declines less. Our results suggest that designing $\\textbf{agent's autonomy}$ that supports $\\textbf{human autonomy}$ (both perceived control and oversight effectiveness) helps users benefit from personalization without being deterred by growing privacy concerns. This positions aligning decision authority between users and agents ($\\textbf{autonomy alignment}$) as an important opportunity and challenge for trustworthy human-agent interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Banayeeanzade","name":"Banayeeanzade, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ala N.","familyName":"Tak","name":"Tak, Ala N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Bahrani","name":"Bahrani, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anahita","familyName":"Bolourani","name":"Bolourani, Anahita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Blas","name":"Blas, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilio","familyName":"Ferrara","name":"Ferrara, Emilio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Gratch","name":"Gratch, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Praneeth","familyName":"Karimireddy","name":"Karimireddy, Sai Praneeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychological Steering in LLMs: An Evaluation of Effectiveness and Trustworthiness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:49:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-02T02:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:19:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ability to control LLMs' emulated emotional states and personality traits is an essential step in enabling rich, human-centered interactions in socially interactive settings. We introduce PsySET, a Psychologically-informed benchmark to evaluate LLM Steering Effectiveness and Trustworthiness across the emotion and personality domains. Our study spans four models from different LLM families paired with various steering strategies, including prompting, fine-tuning, and representation engineering. Our results indicate that prompting is consistently effective but limited in intensity control, whereas vector injections achieve finer controllability while slightly reducing output quality. Moreover, we explore the trustworthiness of steered LLMs by assessing safety, truthfulness, fairness, and ethics, highlighting potential side effects and behavioral shifts. Notably, we observe idiosyncratic effects; for instance, even a positive emotion like joy can degrade robustness to adversarial factuality, lower privacy awareness, and increase preferential bias. Meanwhile, anger predictably elevates toxicity yet strengthens leakage resistance. Our framework establishes the first holistic evaluation of emotion and personality steering, offering insights into its interpretability and reliability for socially interactive applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026. Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:38Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Low-Precision Transformer Training Fails: An Analysis on Flash Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:01:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T11:14:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-18T01:36:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:24:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-14T08:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:53:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The pursuit of computational efficiency has driven the adoption of low-precision formats for training transformer models. However, this progress is often hindered by notorious training instabilities. This paper provides the first mechanistic explanation for a long-standing and unresolved failure case where training with flash attention in low-precision settings leads to catastrophic loss explosion. Our in-depth analysis reveals that the failure is not a random artifact but caused by two intertwined phenomena: the emergence of similar low-rank representations within the attention mechanism and the compounding effect of biased rounding errors inherent in low-precision arithmetic. We demonstrate how these factors create a vicious cycle of error accumulation that corrupts weight updates, ultimately derailing the training dynamics. To validate our findings, we introduce a minimal modification to the flash attention that mitigates the bias in rounding errors. This simple change stabilizes the training process, confirming our analysis and offering a practical solution to this persistent problem. Code is available at https://github.com/ucker/why-low-precision-training-fails."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puzhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Puzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing coherent spatial memory in LLM agents through graph rectification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:27:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T23:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a map description through global traversal navigation instructions, an LLM can often infer the implicit spatial layout and answer user queries by providing shortest paths. However, such context-dependent querying becomes incapable as environments grow larger, motivating the need for incremental map construction that builds a complete topological graph from stepwise observations. We propose LLM-MapRepair, a framework for LLM-driven construction and map repair, designed to detect, localize, and correct structural inconsistencies in incrementally constructed navigation graphs. Our contributions include a Version Control mechanism for graph construction, an Edge Impact Score for repair prioritization, and a cleaned variant of the MANGO benchmark tailored for LLM-driven map construction and repair. We evaluate the framework on four evaluation settings: a synthetic per-component ablation (gpt-4.1, n=20 seeds per cell), a cross-vendor sweep over seven LLMs from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google on both synthetic and TextWorld procedurally-generated text-adventure games, a repair-stage evaluation on all 42 cleaned-MANGO games with non-zero residual conflicts (534 conflicts; three vendors x three modes plus two non-LLM references), and an end-to-end natural-text deployment on Chapters 16-17 of Dream of the Red Chamber. On the DRC deployment, LLM-MapRepair achieves 94.3% node recall (+8.6 pp over direct LLM mapping) and 88.2% edge recall (+55.8 pp), using GPT-4.1; the recall improvements come with predicted node and edge counts that are roughly 4x the ground-truth counts (Table 4), reflecting the discretization-driven over-generation trade-off we discuss in the Limitations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Pang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chung-Pang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Changwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soofiyan","familyName":"Atar","name":"Atar, Soofiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Richter","name":"Richter, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Yip","name":"Yip, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feedback Matters: Augmenting Autonomous Dissection with Visual and Topological Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:26:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-29T20:05:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:12:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous surgical systems must adapt to highly dynamic environments where tissue properties and visual cues evolve rapidly. Central to such adaptability is feedback: the ability to sense, interpret, and respond to changes during execution. While feedback mechanisms have been explored in surgical robotics, ranging from tool and tissue tracking to error detection, existing methods remain limited in handling the topological and perceptual challenges of tissue dissection. In this work, we propose a feedback-enabled framework for autonomous tissue dissection that explicitly reasons about topological changes from endoscopic images after each dissection action. This structured feedback guides subsequent actions, enabling the system to localize dissection progress and adapt policies online. To improve the reliability of such feedback, we introduce visibility metrics that quantify tissue exposure and formulate optimal controller designs that actively manipulate tissue to maximize visibility. Finally, we integrate these feedback mechanisms with both planning-based and learning-based dissection methods, and demonstrate experimentally that they significantly enhance autonomy, reduce errors, and improve robustness in complex surgical scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Hariri","name":"Hariri, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Samandar","name":"Samandar, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Hinczewski","name":"Hinczewski, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vipin","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Vipin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Pass@k: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:14:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-24T05:18:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-25T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T23:24:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:09:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:55:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pass$@k$ is widely used to report the reasoning performance of LLMs, but it often produces unstable and potentially misleading rankings, especially when the number of trials (samples) is limited and computational resources are constrained. We present a principled Bayesian evaluation framework that replaces Pass$@k$ and average accuracy over $N$ trials (avg$@N$) with posterior estimates of a model's underlying success probability and credible intervals, yielding stable rankings and a transparent decision rule for differences. Evaluation outcomes are modeled as categorical (not just 0/1) with a Dirichlet prior, giving closed-form expressions for the posterior mean and uncertainty of any weighted rubric and enabling the use of prior evidence when appropriate. Theoretically, under a uniform prior, the Bayesian posterior mean is order-equivalent to average accuracy (Pass$@1$), explaining its empirical robustness while adding principled uncertainty. Empirically, in simulations with known ground-truth success rates and on AIME'24/'25, HMMT'25, and BrUMO'25, the posterior-based procedure achieves faster convergence and greater rank stability than Pass$@k$ and recent variants, enabling reliable comparisons at far smaller sample counts. The framework clarifies when observed gaps are statistically meaningful (non-overlapping credible intervals) versus noise, and it naturally extends to graded, rubric-based evaluations. Together, these results recommend replacing Pass$@k$ for LLM evaluation and ranking with a posterior-based, compute-efficient protocol that unifies binary and non-binary evaluation while making uncertainty explicit. Source code is available at https://github.com/mohsenhariri/scorio"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"OpenReview (ICLR 2026): https://openreview.net/forum?id=PTXi3Ef4sT"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"En","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Turning Drift into Constraint: Robust Reasoning Alignment in Non-Stationary Multi-Stream Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-02T06:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:23:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T11:36:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper identifies a critical yet underexplored challenge in reasoning alignment from multiple multi-modal large language models (MLLMs): In non-stationary environments, the diverse reasoning distributions of source models often evolve unpredictably, transmitting systematic biases and drift to the target model. To address this, we formulate multi-source reasoning alignment as a constraint satisfaction problem under concept drift theory. We propose Autonomous Preference Optimization (APO), a novel framework that treats inter-model divergences not as noise, but as dynamic negative constraints. APO operates via a two-stage protocol: first, supervised bootstrapping projects the target model into the capability union of source models; second, constraint-aware optimization synthesizes a consistent consensus manifold by explicitly suppressing drifting trajectories via a multi-negative Plackett-Luce objective. Extensive experiments on chest X-ray interpretation demonstrate that our 7B model achieves superior robustness, outperforming even proprietary source models in average accuracy. Furthermore, we release CXR-MAX, a large-scale benchmark comprising 170,982 reasoning trajectories from seven large-scale MLLMs to facilitate research on reasoning alignment under drift. Code and data are available at: https://github.com/XiaoyuYoung/APO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Luhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T14:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T16:53:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-30T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We deploy large language models (LLMs) as business development (BD) agents for persuasive price negotiation in online travel agencies (OTAs). The agent must follow a multi-stage Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) and strict guardrails (no over-promising and no hallucinations), while remaining human-like and effective over long, multi-turn dialogues. We propose Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization (REPO), a reinforcement learning post-training method that combines heterogeneous rewards: a preference-trained reward model (RM), an LLM-as-a-judge (RJ) for nuanced behaviors (e.g., emotional value and SOP compliance), and rule-based reward functions (RF) (mainly regex-based) for deterministic checks on numerics, formatting, and guardrails. In expert consensus evaluation (three human experts; 30 online conversations and 45 curated bad cases), REPO improves average dialogue rating to 4.63 (+0.33 over GRPO) and raises the share of conversations with at least one excellent response to 66.67% (+23.34 pp over GRPO), while achieving a 93.33% bad-case fix rate with 75.56% clean fixes. In a production A/B test on 9,653 real customer conversations (vs. an intent-driven dialogue system), REPO improves response rate by +12.14 pp and task success rate by +5.94 pp (p\u0026lt;0.001)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by ACL 2026 indusry track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naimeng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Naimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Liargkovas","name":"Liargkovas, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yunan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostis","familyName":"Kaffes","name":"Kaffes, Kostis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speculative Actions: A Lossless Framework for Faster Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:28:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-23T17:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T01:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are increasingly deployed in complex, interactive environments, yet their runtime remains a major bottleneck for training, evaluation, and real-world use. Typical agent behavior unfolds sequentially, with each action requiring an API call that can incur substantial latency. For example, a game of chess between two state-of-the-art agents can take hours. We introduce Speculative Actions, a lossless acceleration framework for general agentic systems. Inspired by speculative execution in microprocessors and speculative decoding in LLM inference, our method uses faster models to predict likely future actions and execute them in parallel, committing only when predictions match. We evaluate speculative actions across gaming, e-commerce, and web search environments, and additionally study a lossy extension in an operating systems setting. Across domains, we achieve up to 55% next-action prediction accuracy, translating into up to 20% latency reductions. Finally, we present a cost-latency analysis that formalizes the tradeoff between speculative breadth and time savings. This analysis enables principled tuning and selective branch launching to ensure that multi-branch speculation delivers practical speedups without prohibitive cost growth."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikun","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijia","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Huijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Locate-Then-Examine: Grounded Region Reasoning Improves Detection of AI-Generated Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.10; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:29:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T18:16:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of AI-generated imagery has blurred the boundary between real and synthetic content, raising practical concerns for digital integrity. Vision-language models (VLMs) can provide natural language explanations, but standard one-pass classifiers often miss subtle artifacts in high-quality synthetic images and offer limited grounding in the pixels. We propose Locate-Then-Examine (LTE), a two-stage VLM-based forensic framework that first localizes suspicious regions and then re-examines these crops together with the full image to refine the real vs. AI-generated verdict and its explanation. LTE explicitly links each decision to localized visual evidence through region proposals and region-aware reasoning. To support training and evaluation, we introduce TRACE, a dataset of 20,000 real and high-quality synthetic images with region-level annotations and automatically generated forensic explanations, constructed by a VLM-based pipeline with additional consistency checks and quality control. Across TRACE and multiple external benchmarks, LTE achieves competitive accuracy and improved robustness while providing human-understandable, region-grounded explanations suitable for forensic deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 11 figures (including supplementary material)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:22:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhodip","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Subhodip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun M","familyName":"S","name":"S, Varun M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyans","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Shreyans","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarthak Kumar","familyName":"Maharana","name":"Maharana, Sarthak Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prathosh A.","familyName":"P","name":"P, Prathosh A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlearning in Diffusion models under Data Constraints: A Variational Inference Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:39:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-10T13:26:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:35:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T16:41:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-23T04:07:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:28:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a responsible and safe deployment of diffusion models in various domains, regulating the generated outputs from these models is desirable because such models could generate undesired, violent, and obscene outputs. To tackle this problem, recent works use machine unlearning methodology to forget training data points containing these undesired features from pre-trained generative models. However, these methods proved to be ineffective in data-constrained settings where the whole training dataset is inaccessible. Thus, the principal objective of this work is to propose a machine unlearning methodology that can prevent the generation of outputs containing undesired features from a pre-trained diffusion model in such a data-constrained setting. Our proposed method, termed as Variational Diffusion Unlearning (VDU), is a computationally efficient method that only requires access to a subset of training data containing undesired features. Our approach is inspired by the variational inference framework with the objective of minimizing a loss function consisting of two terms: plasticity inducer and stability regularizer. Plasticity inducer reduces the log-likelihood of the undesired training data points, while the stability regularizer, essential for preventing loss of image generation quality, regularizes the model in parameter space. We validate the effectiveness of our method through comprehensive experiments for both class unlearning and feature unlearning. For class unlearning, we unlearn some user-identified classes from MNIST, CIFAR-10, and tinyImageNet datasets from a pre-trained unconditional denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM). Similarly, for feature unlearning, we unlearn the generation of certain high-level features from a pre-trained Stable Diffusion model trained on LAION-5B dataset."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengpu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shengpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Minjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slow-Fast Policy Optimization: Reposition-Before-Update for LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:24:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T05:57:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:07:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T22:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:26:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become central to enhancing reasoning in large language models (LLMs). Yet on-policy algorithms such as Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) often suffer in early training: noisy gradients from low-quality rollouts lead to unstable updates and inefficient exploration. We introduce Slow-Fast Policy Optimization (SFPO), a simple yet efficient framework to address the above limitations via decomposing each step into three stages: a short fast trajectory of inner steps on the same batch, a reposition mechanism to control off-policy drift, and a final slow correction. This reposition-before-update design preserves the objective and rollout process unchanged, making SFPO plug-compatible with existing policy-gradient pipelines. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SFPO consistently improves stability, reduces number of rollouts, and accelerates convergence of reasoning RL training. Specifically, it outperforms GRPO by up to 2.80 points in average on math reasoning benchmarks. It also achieves up to 4.93\\texttimes{} fewer rollouts and an up to 4.19\\texttimes{} reduction in wall-clock time to match GRPO's best accuracy. Project website is available at https://slow-fast-po.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunghwei","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yunghwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doctor-R1: Mastering Clinical Inquiry with Experiential Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:54:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T06:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T08:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The professionalism of a human doctor in outpatient service depends on two core abilities: the ability to make accurate medical decisions and the medical consultation skill to conduct strategic, empathetic patient inquiry. Existing Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable accuracy on medical decision-making benchmarks. However, they often lack the ability to conduct the strategic and empathetic consultation, which is essential for real-world clinical scenarios. To address this gap, we propose Doctor-R1, an AI doctor agent trained to master both of the capabilities by ask high-yield questions and conduct strategic multi-turn inquiry to guide decision-making. Our framework introduces three key components: a multi-agent interactive environment, a two-tiered reward architecture that separately optimizes clinical decision-making and communicative inquiry skills, and an experience repository to ground policy learning in high-quality prior trajectories. We evaluate Doctor-R1 on OpenAI's HealthBench and MAQuE, assessed across multi-facet metrics, such as communication quality, user experience, and task accuracy. Remarkably, Doctor-R1 surpasses state-of-the-art open-source specialized LLMs by a substantial margin with higher parameter efficiency and outperforms powerful proprietary models. Furthermore, the human expert evaluations show that Doctor-R1 achieves superior clinical capability and patient-centric performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of the framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04504","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04504","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04504","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijing","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunze","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Yunze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengda","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junkun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Junkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asynchronous Denoising Diffusion Models for Aligning Text-to-Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:45:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T10:10:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:37:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved impressive results in generating high-quality images. Yet, they often struggle to faithfully align the generated images with the input prompts. This limitation is associated with synchronous denoising, where all pixels simultaneously evolve from random noise to clear images. As a result, during generation, the prompt-related regions can only reference the unrelated regions at the same noise level, failing to obtain clear context and ultimately impairing text-to-image alignment. To address this issue, we propose asynchronous diffusion models -- a novel framework that allocates distinct timesteps to different pixels and reformulates the pixel-wise denoising process. By dynamically modulating the timestep schedules of individual pixels, prompt-related regions are denoised more gradually than unrelated regions, thereby allowing them to leverage clearer inter-pixel context. Consequently, these prompt-related regions achieve better alignment in the final images. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our asynchronous diffusion models can significantly improve text-to-image alignment across diverse prompts. The code repository for this work is available at https://github.com/hu-zijing/AsynDM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026, 25 pages, 13 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04504","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Nekoei","name":"Nekoei, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrice","familyName":"Bechard","name":"Bechard, Patrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleh","familyName":"Shliazhko","name":"Shliazhko, Oleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orlando Marquez","familyName":"Ayala","name":"Ayala, Orlando Marquez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Caccia","name":"Caccia, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drouin","name":"Drouin, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JEF-Hinter: Leveraging Offline Knowledge for Improving Web Agents Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T19:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:04:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents perform well in sequential decision-making tasks, but improving them on unfamiliar domains often requires costly online interactions or fine-tuning on large expert datasets. These strategies are impractical for closed-source models and expensive for open-source ones, with risks of catastrophic forgetting. Offline trajectories offer reusable knowledge, yet demonstration-based methods struggle because raw traces are long, noisy, and tied to specific tasks. We present Just-in-time Episodic Feedback Hinter (JEF-Hinter), an agentic system that distills offline traces into compact, context-aware hints. A zooming mechanism highlights decisive steps in long trajectories, capturing both strategies and pitfalls. Unlike prior methods, JEF-Hinter leverages both successful and failed trajectories, extracting guidance even when only failure data is available, while supporting parallelized hint generation and benchmark-independent prompting. At inference, a retriever selects relevant hints for the current state, providing targeted guidance with transparency and traceability. Experiments on MiniWoB++, WorkArena-L1, and WebArena-Lite show that JEF-Hinter consistently outperforms strong baselines, including human- and document-based hints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04217","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04217","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04217","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Chenlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leheng","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Leheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yancheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MLLMEraser: Achieving Test-Time Unlearning in Multimodal Large Language Models through Activation Steering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T11:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:44:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T12:41:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across vision-language tasks, yet their large-scale deployment raises pressing concerns about memorized private data, outdated knowledge, and harmful content. Existing unlearning approaches for MLLMs typically adapt training-based strategies such as gradient ascent or preference optimization, but these methods are computationally expensive, irreversible, and often distort retained knowledge. In this work, we propose MLLMEraser, an input-aware, training-free framework for test-time unlearning. Our approach leverages activation steering to enable dynamic knowledge erasure without parameter updates. Specifically, we construct a multimodal erasure direction by contrasting adversarially perturbed, knowledge-recall image-text pairs with knowledge-erasure counterparts, capturing both textual and visual discrepancies. To prevent unnecessary interference, we further design an input-aware steering mechanism that adaptively determines when and how the erasure direction should be applied, preserving utility on retained knowledge while enforcing forgetting on designated content. Experiments on LLaVA-1.5 and Qwen-2.5-VL demonstrate that MLLMEraser consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MLLM unlearning baselines, achieving stronger forgetting performance with lower computational cost and minimal utility degradation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04217","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:40:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04347","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindya Sundar","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Anindya Sundar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monowar","familyName":"Bhuyan","name":"Bhuyan, Monowar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unmasking Backdoors: An Explainable Defense via Gradient-Attention Anomaly Scoring for Pre-trained Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T11:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained language models have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, particularly when fine-tuned on large, domain-relevant datasets. However, they remain vulnerable to backdoor attacks, where adversaries embed malicious behaviors using trigger patterns in the training data. These triggers remain dormant during normal usage, but, when activated, can cause targeted misclassifications. In this work, we investigate the internal behavior of backdoored pre-trained encoder-based language models, focusing on the consistent shift in attention and gradient attribution when processing poisoned inputs; where the trigger token dominates both attention and gradient signals, overriding the surrounding context. We propose an inference-time defense that constructs anomaly scores by combining token-level attention and gradient information. Extensive experiments on text classification tasks across diverse backdoor attack scenarios demonstrate that our method significantly reduces attack success rates compared to existing baselines. Furthermore, we provide an interpretability-driven analysis of the scoring mechanism, shedding light on trigger localization and the robustness of the proposed defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages total (9 pages main text + 6 pages appendix + references), 16 figures. Preprint version; the final camera-ready version may differ. Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04347","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wengao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wengao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianlei","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Lianlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time Reasoning Enhancement via Latent Thought Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:50:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-07T17:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:57:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T21:10:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-26T14:30:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:31:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shifted from explicit Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to more efficient latent reasoning, where intermediate thoughts are represented as vectors rather than text. However, latent reasoning can be brittle on challenging, out-of-distribution tasks where robust reasoning is most critical. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Latent Thought Policy Optimization (LTPO), a parameter-free framework that enhances LLM reasoning entirely at test time, without requiring model parameter updates. LTPO treats intermediate latent \"thought\" vectors as dynamic parameters that are actively optimized for each problem instance. It employs an online policy gradient method guided by an intrinsic, confidence-based reward signal computed directly from the frozen LLM's own output distributions, eliminating the need for external supervision or expensive text generation during optimization. Extensive experiments on five reasoning benchmarks show that LTPO not only matches or surpasses strong baselines on standard tasks but also demonstrates remarkable robustness where others fail. Most notably, on highly challenging AIME benchmarks where existing latent reasoning baselines collapse to near-zero accuracy, LTPO delivers substantial improvements, showcasing a unique capability for complex reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soufiane","familyName":"Amini","name":"Amini, Soufiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yassine","familyName":"Benajiba","name":"Benajiba, Yassine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesare","familyName":"Bernardis","name":"Bernardis, Cesare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Cayet","name":"Cayet, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Chafi","name":"Chafi, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrahim","familyName":"Fathan","name":"Fathan, Abderrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Faucon","name":"Faucon, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Hilloulin","name":"Hilloulin, Damien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungpack","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungpack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingo","familyName":"Kossyk","name":"Kossyk, Ingo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tran Minh Son","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Tran Minh Son","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rhicheek","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Rhicheek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Schweizer","name":"Schweizer, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jyotika","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Jyotika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shailender","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Shailender","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Talamadupula","name":"Talamadupula, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec): A Unified Representation for AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:26:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:29:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T11:55:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-07T14:02:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:41:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of agent frameworks has led to fragmentation in how agents are defined, executed, and evaluated. Existing systems differ in their abstractions, data flow semantics, and tool integrations, making it difficult to share or reproduce workflows. We introduce Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec), a declarative language that defines AI agents and agentic workflows in a way that is compatible across frameworks, promoting reusability, portability and interoperability of AI agents. Agent Spec defines a common set of components, control and data flow semantics, and schemas that allow an agent to be defined once and executed across different runtimes. Agent Spec also introduces a standardized Evaluation harness to assess agent behavior and agentic workflows across runtimes - analogous to how HELM and related harnesses standardized LLM evaluation - so that performance, robustness, and efficiency can be compared consistently across frameworks. We demonstrate this using four distinct runtimes (LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen, and WayFlow) evaluated over three different benchmarks (SimpleQA Verified, $τ^2$-Bench and BIRD-SQL). We provide accompanying toolsets: a Python SDK (PyAgentSpec), a reference runtime (WayFlow), and adapters for popular frameworks (e.g., LangGraph, AutoGen, CrewAI). Agent Spec bridges the gap between model-centric and agent-centric standardization \u0026amp; evaluation, laying the groundwork for reliable, reusable, and portable agentic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-10T02:49:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Woodruff","name":"Woodruff, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niels","familyName":"Warncke","name":"Warncke, Niels","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Jose","name":"Jose, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Riché","name":"Riché, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mia","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Mia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inoculation Prompting: Eliciting traits from LLMs during training can suppress them at test-time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:04:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T03:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T08:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-03T12:21:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:33:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language model finetuning often results in learning undesirable traits in combination with desired ones. To address this, we propose inoculation prompting: modifying finetuning data by prepending a short system-prompt instruction that deliberately elicits the undesirable trait. At test time, we evaluate without the instruction; inoculated models have much lower expression of the trait than models trained with unmodified training data. Inoculation is selective: in a toy setting where assistant responses are always in Spanish and ALL-CAPS, an appropriate inoculation (e.g., ``You always speak in Spanish.'') teaches the model to capitalize responses while still responding in English. We find that inoculation is also effective across several additional settings: reducing emergent misalignment (EM) from task-specific finetuning, defending against backdoor injections, and mitigating the transmission of traits via subliminal learning. Follow-up analysis suggests a mechanism: making a trait less surprising via inoculation reduces optimization pressure to globally update the model, thereby reducing the degree of generalization. Our analysis relates to prior work on EM: inoculation explains prior findings that educational contexts mitigate EM from insecure code. Beyond demonstrating a simple and effective technique for selective learning, our results contribute to a better conceptual understanding of how and why language models generalize."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages, 22 figures. Under review at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:57:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Om","familyName":"Tailor","name":"Tailor, Om","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audit the Whisper: Detecting Steganographic Collusion in Multi-Agent LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:51:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T19:12:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:07:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent deployments of large language models (LLMs) are increasingly embedded in market, allocation, and governance workflows, yet covert coordination among agents can silently erode trust and social welfare. Existing audits are dominated by heuristics that lack theoretical guarantees, struggle to transfer across tasks, and seldom ship with the infrastructure needed for independent replication. We introduce Audit the Whisper, a conference-grade research artifact that spans theory, benchmark design, detection, and reproducibility. Our contributions are: (i) a channel-capacity analysis showing how interventions such as paraphrase, rate limiting, and role permutation impose quantifiable capacity penalties-operationalised via paired-run Kullback--Leibler diagnostics-that tighten mutual-information thresholds with finite-sample guarantees and full proofs; (ii) ColludeBench-v0, covering pricing, first-price auctions, peer review, and hosted Gemini/Groq APIs with configurable covert schemes, deterministic manifests, and reward instrumentation; and (iii) a calibrated auditing pipeline that fuses cross-run mutual information, permutation invariance, watermark variance, and fairness-aware acceptance bias, each tuned to a $10^{-3}$ false-positive budget and validated by 10k honest runs plus an e-value martingale. Across ColludeBench and external suites including Secret Collusion, CASE, Perfect Collusion Benchmark, and SentinelAgent, the union meta-test attains state-of-the-art power at fixed FPR while ablations surface price-of-auditing trade-offs and fairness-driven colluders invisible to MI alone. We release regeneration scripts, anonymized manifests, and documentation so that external auditors can reproduce every figure, satisfy double-blind requirements, and extend the framework with minimal effort."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 0 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Edward Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ethan Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Collaborative Intelligence: Dual-Dial Control for Reliable LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate often wastes compute by using a fixed adversarial stance, aggregating without deliberation, or stopping on heuristics. We introduce MACI, an active controller with two independent dials that decouple information from behavior: an information dial that gates evidence by quality, and a behavior dial that schedules contentiousness from exploration to consolidation. A moderator tracks disagreement, overlap, evidence quality, and argument quality, and halts when gains plateau. We provide theory-lite guarantees for nonincreasing dispersion and provable termination, with a budget-feasible scheduler. Across clinical diagnosis and news-bias tasks, MACI improves accuracy and calibration while reducing tokens, and converts residual uncertainty into precision RAG plans that specify what to retrieve next. We use a cross-family LLM judge (CRIT) as a conservative soft weight and stop signal, validated for order invariance and judge-swap stability; stability depends on using high-capability judges. MACI turns debate into a budget-aware, measurable, and provably terminating controller."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 5 figures, 21 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soo Yong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Soo Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Suin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent-Daniel","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Vincent-Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeongyeon","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Gyeongyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedCLM: Learning to Localize and Reason via a CoT-Curriculum in Medical Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:26:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bridging clinical diagnostic reasoning with AI remains a central challenge in medical imaging. We introduce MedCLM, an automated pipeline that converts detection datasets into large-scale medical visual question answering (VQA) data with Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning by linking lesion boxes to organ segmentation and structured rationales. These contextual signals enable medical vision-language models to generate question-answer pairs with step-by-step reasoning. To utilize this data effectively, we propose an Integrated CoT-Curriculum Strategy composed of an Easy stage with explicit lesion boxes for visual grounding, a Medium stage that encourages implicit localization, and a Hard stage for weakly supervised reasoning. Experimental results demonstrate that MedCLM attains state-of-the-art performance on several medical VQA benchmarks, providing a scalable framework for developing clinically aligned medical vision-language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04454","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04454","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04454","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiangchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dachuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saayan","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Saayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:01:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:12:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) show strong reasoning abilities, often amplified by Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting and reinforcement learning (RL). Although RL algorithms can substantially improve reasoning, they struggle to expand reasoning boundaries because they learn from their own reasoning trajectories rather than acquiring external knowledge. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) offers complementary benefits but typically requires large-scale data and risks overfitting. Recent attempts to combine SFT and RL face three main challenges: data inefficiency, algorithm-specific designs, and catastrophic forgetting. We propose a plug-and-play framework that dynamically integrates SFT into RL by selecting challenging examples for SFT. This approach reduces SFT data requirements and remains agnostic to the choice of RL or SFT algorithm. To mitigate catastrophic forgetting of RL-acquired skills during SFT, we select high-entropy tokens for loss calculation and freeze parameters identified as critical for RL. Our method achieves state-of-the-art (SoTA) reasoning performance using only 1.5% of the SFT data and 20.4% of the RL data used by prior SoTA, providing an efficient and plug-and-play solution for combining SFT and RL in reasoning post-training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04454","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenmei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhenmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Vision-Language Model Can't Even Count to 20: Exposing the Failures of VLMs in Compositional Counting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T00:11:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:09:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have become a central focus of today's AI community, owing to their impressive abilities gained from training on large-scale vision-language data from the Web. These models have demonstrated strong performance across diverse tasks, including image understanding, video understanding, complex visual reasoning, and embodied AI. Despite these noteworthy successes, a fundamental question remains: Can VLMs count objects correctly? In this paper, we introduce a simple yet effective benchmark, VLMCountBench, designed under a minimalist setting with only basic geometric shapes (e.g., triangles, circles) and their compositions, focusing exclusively on counting tasks without interference from other factors. We adopt strict independent variable control and systematically study the effects of simple properties such as color, size, and prompt refinement in a controlled ablation. Our empirical results reveal that while VLMs can count reliably when only one shape type is present, they exhibit substantial failures when multiple shape types are combined (i.e., compositional counting). This highlights a fundamental empirical limitation of current VLMs and motivates important directions for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Hamman","name":"Hamman, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghamitra","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sanghamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Consistency in Retrieval-Augmented Systems with Group Similarity Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:14:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RAG systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains where users expect outputs to be consistent across semantically equivalent queries. However, existing systems often exhibit significant inconsistencies due to variability in both the retriever and generator (LLM), undermining trust and reliability. In this work, we focus on information consistency, i.e., the requirement that outputs convey the same core content across semantically equivalent inputs. We introduce a principled evaluation framework that decomposes RAG consistency into retriever-level, generator-level, and end-to-end components, helping identify inconsistency sources. To improve consistency, we propose Paraphrased Set Group Relative Policy Optimization (PS-GRPO), an RL approach that leverages multiple rollouts across paraphrased set to assign group similarity rewards. We leverage PS-GRPO to achieve Information Consistent RAG (Con-RAG), training the generator to produce consistent outputs across paraphrased queries and remain robust to retrieval-induced variability. Because exact reward computation over paraphrase sets is computationally expensive, we also introduce a scalable approximation method that retains effectiveness while enabling efficient, large-scale training. Empirical evaluations across short-form, multi-hop, and long-form QA benchmarks demonstrate that Con-RAG significantly improves both consistency and accuracy over strong baselines, even in the absence of explicit ground-truth supervision. Our work provides practical solutions for evaluating and building reliable RAG systems for safety-critical deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Reliable ML from Unreliable Data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04365","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04365","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04365","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shucheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sikai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion^2: Dual Diffusion Model with Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Noise for Momentary Trajectory Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:19:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate pedestrian trajectory prediction is crucial for ensuring safety and efficiency in autonomous driving and human-robot interaction scenarios. Earlier studies primarily utilized sufficient observational data to predict future trajectories. However, in real-world scenarios, such as pedestrians suddenly emerging from blind spots, sufficient observational data is often unavailable (i.e. momentary trajectory), making accurate prediction challenging and increasing the risk of traffic accidents. Therefore, advancing research on pedestrian trajectory prediction under extreme scenarios is critical for enhancing traffic safety. In this work, we propose a novel framework termed Diffusion^2, tailored for momentary trajectory prediction. Diffusion^2 consists of two sequentially connected diffusion models: one for backward prediction, which generates unobserved historical trajectories, and the other for forward prediction, which forecasts future trajectories. Given that the generated unobserved historical trajectories may introduce additional noise, we propose a dual-head parameterization mechanism to estimate their aleatoric uncertainty and design a temporally adaptive noise module that dynamically modulates the noise scale in the forward diffusion process. Empirically, Diffusion^2 sets a new state-of-the-art in momentary trajectory prediction on ETH/UCY and Stanford Drone datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 7 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04365","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04360","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04360","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04360","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Early Exploration of Deep-Learning-Driven Prefetching for Far Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:59:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Far-memory systems, where applications store less-active data in more energy-efficient memory media, are increasingly adopted by data centers. However, applications are bottlenecked by on-demand data fetching from far- to local-memory. We present Memix, a far-memory system that embodies a deep-learning-system co-design for efficient and accurate prefetching, minimizing on-demand far-memory accesses. One key observation is that memory accesses are shaped by both application semantics and runtime context, providing an opportunity to optimize each independently. Preliminary evaluation of Memix on data-intensive workloads shows that it outperforms the state-of-the-art far-memory system by up to 42%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04360","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04320","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04320","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04320","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeru","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zeru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanshu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixiang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Read the Scene, Not the Script: Outcome-Aware Safety for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:46:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safety-aligned Large Language Models (LLMs) still show two dominant failure modes: they are easily jailbroken, or they over-refuse harmless inputs that contain sensitive surface signals. We trace both to a common cause: current models reason weakly about links between actions and outcomes and over-rely on surface-form signals, lexical or stylistic cues that do not encode consequences. We define this failure mode as Consequence-blindness. To study consequence-blindness, we build a benchmark named CB-Bench covering four risk scenarios that vary whether semantic risk aligns with outcome risk, enabling evaluation under both matched and mismatched conditions which are often ignored by existing safety benchmarks. Mainstream models consistently fail to separate these risks and exhibit consequence-blindness, indicating that consequence-blindness is widespread and systematic. To mitigate consequence-blindness, we introduce CS-Chain-4k, a consequence-reasoning dataset for safety alignment. Models fine-tuned on CS-Chain-4k show clear gains against semantic-camouflage jailbreaks and reduce over-refusal on harmless inputs, while maintaining utility and generalization on other benchmarks. These results clarify the limits of current alignment, establish consequence-aware reasoning as a core alignment goal and provide a more practical and reproducible evaluation path."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04320","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yucong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mashrur","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Mashrur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongkai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FairAgent: Democratizing Fairness-Aware Machine Learning with LLM-Powered Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training fair and unbiased machine learning models is crucial for high-stakes applications, yet it presents significant challenges. Effective bias mitigation requires deep expertise in fairness definitions, metrics, data preprocessing, and machine learning techniques. In addition, the complex process of balancing model performance with fairness requirements while properly handling sensitive attributes makes fairness-aware model development inaccessible to many practitioners. To address these challenges, we introduce FairAgent, an LLM-powered automated system that significantly simplifies fairness-aware model development. FairAgent eliminates the need for deep technical expertise by automatically analyzing datasets for potential biases, handling data preprocessing and feature engineering, and implementing appropriate bias mitigation strategies based on user requirements. Our experiments demonstrate that FairAgent achieves significant performance improvements while significantly reducing development time and expertise requirements, making fairness-aware machine learning more accessible to practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICDM 2025 Demo Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huidong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Keyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Importance of Task Complexity in Evaluating LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:08:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model multi-agent systems (LLM-MAS) offer a promising paradigm for harnessing collective intelligence to achieve more advanced forms of AI behaviour. While recent studies suggest that LLM-MAS can outperform LLM single-agent systems (LLM-SAS) on certain tasks, the lack of systematic experimental designs limits the strength and generality of these conclusions. We argue that a principled understanding of task complexity, such as the degree of sequential reasoning required and the breadth of capabilities involved, is essential for assessing the effectiveness of LLM-MAS in task solving. To this end, we propose a theoretical framework characterising tasks along two dimensions: depth, representing reasoning length, and width, representing capability diversity. We theoretically examine a representative class of LLM-MAS, namely the multi-agent debate system, and empirically evaluate its performance in both discriminative and generative tasks with varying depth and width. Theoretical and empirical results show that the benefit of LLM-MAS over LLM-SAS increases with both task depth and width, and the effect is more pronounced with respect to depth. This clarifies when LLM-MAS are beneficial and provides a principled foundation for designing future LLM-MAS methods and benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingnan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lingnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Zhengyong","name":"Zhengyong, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanqing","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equipping Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models with Document Structure Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:04:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities, their reliance on parametric knowledge often leads to factual inaccuracies. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this by leveraging external documents, yet existing approaches treat retrieved passages as isolated chunks, ignoring valuable structure that is crucial for document organization. Motivated by this gap, we propose Retrieve-DocumentRoute-Read (RDR2), a novel framework that explicitly incorporates structural information throughout the RAG process. RDR2 employs an LLM-based router to dynamically navigate document structure trees, jointly evaluating content relevance and hierarchical relationships to assemble optimal evidence. Our key innovation lies in formulating document routing as a trainable task, with automatic action curation and structure-aware passage selection inspired by human reading strategies. Through comprehensive evaluation on five challenging datasets, RDR2 achieves state-of-the-art performance, demonstrating that explicit structural awareness significantly enhances RAG systems' ability to acquire and utilize knowledge, particularly in complex scenarios requiring multi-document synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentTypo: Adaptive Typographic Prompt Injection Attacks against Black-box Multimodal Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal agents built on large vision-language models (LVLMs) are increasingly deployed in open-world settings but remain highly vulnerable to prompt injection, especially through visual inputs. We introduce AgentTypo, a black-box red-teaming framework that mounts adaptive typographic prompt injection by embedding optimized text into webpage images. Our automatic typographic prompt injection (ATPI) algorithm maximizes prompt reconstruction by substituting captioners while minimizing human detectability via a stealth loss, with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator guiding black-box optimization over text placement, size, and color. To further enhance attack strength, we develop AgentTypo-pro, a multi-LLM system that iteratively refines injection prompts using evaluation feedback and retrieves successful past examples for continual learning. Effective prompts are abstracted into generalizable strategies and stored in a strategy repository, enabling progressive knowledge accumulation and reuse in future attacks. Experiments on the VWA-Adv benchmark across Classifieds, Shopping, and Reddit scenarios show that AgentTypo significantly outperforms the latest image-based attacks such as AgentAttack. On GPT-4o agents, our image-only attack raises the success rate from 0.23 to 0.45, with consistent results across GPT-4V, GPT-4o-mini, Gemini 1.5 Pro, and Claude 3 Opus. In image+text settings, AgentTypo achieves 0.68 ASR, also outperforming the latest baselines. Our findings reveal that AgentTypo poses a practical and potent threat to multimodal agents and highlight the urgent need for effective defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 8 figures. Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics \u0026amp; Security"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiwon","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Huiwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihyun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Sihyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heeseung","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Heeseung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojin","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextVLA: Vision-Language-Action Model with Amortized Multi-Frame Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:29:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging temporal context is crucial for success in partially observable robotic tasks. However, prior work in behavior cloning has demonstrated inconsistent performance gains when using multi-frame observations. In this paper, we introduce ContextVLA, a policy model that robustly improves robotic task performance by effectively leveraging multi-frame observations. Our approach is motivated by the key observation that Vision-Language-Action models (VLA), i.e., policy models built upon a Vision-Language Model (VLM), more effectively utilize multi-frame observations for action generation. This suggests that VLMs' inherent temporal understanding capability enables them to extract more meaningful context from multi-frame observations. However, the high dimensionality of video inputs introduces significant computational overhead, making VLA training and inference inefficient. To address this, ContextVLA compresses past observations into a single context token, allowing the policy to efficiently leverage temporal context for action generation. Our experiments show that ContextVLA consistently improves over single-frame VLAs and achieves the benefits of full multi-frame training but with reduced training and inference times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://huiwon-jang.github.io/contextvla"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Runhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haldun","familyName":"Balim","name":"Balim, Haldun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible Locomotion Learning with Diffusion Model Predictive Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Legged locomotion demands controllers that are both robust and adaptable, while remaining compatible with task and safety considerations. However, model-free reinforcement learning (RL) methods often yield a fixed policy that can be difficult to adapt to new behaviors at test time. In contrast, Model Predictive Control (MPC) provides a natural approach to flexible behavior synthesis by incorporating different objectives and constraints directly into its optimization process. However, classical MPC relies on accurate dynamics models, which are often difficult to obtain in complex environments and typically require simplifying assumptions. We present Diffusion-MPC, which leverages a learned generative diffusion model as an approximate dynamics prior for planning, enabling flexible test-time adaptation through reward and constraint based optimization. Diffusion-MPC jointly predicts future states and actions; at each reverse step, we incorporate reward planning and impose constraint projection, yielding trajectories that satisfy task objectives while remaining within physical limits. To obtain a planning model that adapts beyond imitation pretraining, we introduce an interactive training algorithm for diffusion based planner: we execute our reward-and-constraint planner in environment, then filter and reweight the collected trajectories by their realized returns before updating the denoiser. Our design enables strong test-time adaptability, allowing the planner to adjust to new reward specifications without retraining. We validate Diffusion-MPC on real world, demonstrating strong locomotion and flexible adaptation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xueqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iat Long","familyName":"Iong","name":"Iong, Iat Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Zehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentRL: Scaling Agentic Reinforcement Learning with a Multi-Turn, Multi-Task Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:40:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have sparked growing interest in building generalist agents that can learn through online interactions. However, applying reinforcement learning (RL) to train LLM agents in multi-turn, multi-task settings remains challenging due to lack of scalable infrastructure and stable training algorithms. In this work, we present the AgentRL framework for scalable multi-turn, multi-task agentic RL training. On the infrastructure side, AgentRL features a fully-asynchronous generation-training pipeline for efficient multi-turn RL. To support heterogeneous environment development in multi-task RL, we design a unified function-call based API interface, containerized environment development, and a centralized controller. On the algorithm side, we propose cross-policy sampling to encourage model exploration in multi-turn settings and task advantage normalization to stabilize multi-task training. Experiments show that AgentRL, trained on open LLMs across five agentic tasks, significantly outperforms GPT-5, Clause-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-R1, and other open-source LLM agents. Multi-task training with AgentRL matches the best results among all task-specific models. AgentRL is open-sourced at https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL. The algorithm and framework are adopted in building \\textsc{\\href{https://autoglm.zhipuai.cn}{AutoGLM}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04202","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Alignment as Predictor of Loss Explosion in Neural Network Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Loss explosions in training deep neural networks can nullify multi-million dollar training runs. Conventional monitoring metrics like weight and gradient norms are often lagging and ambiguous predictors, as their values vary dramatically across different models and even between layers of the same model, making it difficult to establish a unified standard for detecting impending failure. We introduce Spectral Alignment (SA), a novel, theoretically-grounded metric that monitors the distributional alignment between layer inputs and the principal singular vectors of weight matrices. We show that a collapse in the sign diversity of this alignment is a powerful early predictor of representational collapse and training divergence. Empirical results on language models demonstrate that monitoring the SA distribution provides a significantly earlier and clearer warning of loss explosions than traditional scalar metrics. SA's low computational overhead makes it a practical tool for safeguarding model training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04202","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aviv","familyName":"Navon","name":"Navon, Aviv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aviv","familyName":"Shamsian","name":"Shamsian, Aviv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neta","familyName":"Glazer","name":"Glazer, Neta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yael","familyName":"Segal-Feldman","name":"Segal-Feldman, Yael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gill","familyName":"Hetz","name":"Hetz, Gill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Keshet","name":"Keshet, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Fetaya","name":"Fetaya, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Drax: Speech Recognition with Discrete Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion and flow-based non-autoregressive (NAR) models have shown strong promise in large language modeling, however, their potential for automatic speech recognition (ASR) remains largely unexplored. We propose Drax, a discrete flow matching framework for ASR that enables efficient parallel decoding. To better align training with inference, we construct an audio-conditioned probability path that guides the model through trajectories resembling likely intermediate inference errors, rather than direct random noise to target transitions. Our theoretical analysis links the generalization gap to divergences between training and inference occupancies, controlled by cumulative velocity errors, thereby motivating our design choice. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that our approach attains recognition accuracy on par with state-of-the-art speech models while offering improved accuracy-efficiency trade-offs, highlighting discrete flow matching as a promising direction for advancing NAR ASR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhi","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Jingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"de la Cal","name":"de la Cal, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Pinna","name":"Pinna, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anisha","familyName":"Uteem","name":"Uteem, Anisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mar","familyName":"Zamorano","name":"Zamorano, Mar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karine","familyName":"Even-Mendoza","name":"Even-Mendoza, Karine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. B.","familyName":"Langdon","name":"Langdon, W. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hector","familyName":"Menendez","name":"Menendez, Hector","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federica","familyName":"Sarro","name":"Sarro, Federica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GA4GC: Greener Agent for Greener Code via Multi-Objective Configuration Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:34:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coding agents powered by LLMs face critical sustainability and scalability challenges in industrial deployment, with single runs consuming over 100k tokens and incurring environmental costs that may exceed optimization benefits. This paper introduces GA4GC, the first framework to systematically optimize coding agent runtime (greener agent) and code performance (greener code) trade-offs by discovering Pareto-optimal agent hyperparameters and prompt templates. Evaluation on the SWE-Perf benchmark demonstrates up to 135x hypervolume improvement, reducing agent runtime by 37.7% while improving correctness. Our findings establish temperature as the most critical hyperparameter, and provide actionable strategies to balance agent sustainability with code optimization effectiveness in industrial deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SSBSE'25 Challenge Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae-Kyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Tae-Kyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Learning of Pose Regression and Denoising Diffusion with Score Scaling Sampling for Category-level 6D Pose Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latest diffusion models have shown promising results in category-level 6D object pose estimation by modeling the conditional pose distribution with depth image input. The existing methods, however, suffer from slow convergence during training, learning its encoder with the diffusion denoising network in end-to-end fashion, and require an additional network that evaluates sampled pose hypotheses to filter out low-quality pose candidates. In this paper, we propose a novel pipeline that tackles these limitations by two key components. First, the proposed method pretrains the encoder with the direct pose regression head, and jointly learns the networks via the regression head and the denoising diffusion head, significantly accelerating training convergence while achieving higher accuracy. Second, sampling guidance via time-dependent score scaling is proposed s.t. the exploration-exploitation trade-off is effectively taken, eliminating the need for the additional evaluation network. The sampling guidance maintains multi-modal characteristics of symmetric objects at early denoising steps while ensuring high-quality pose generation at final steps. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmarks including REAL275, HouseCat6D, and ROPE, demonstrate that the proposed method, simple yet effective, achieves state-of-the-art accuracies even with single-pose inference, while being more efficient in both training and inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunming","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Kunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaicheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Efficient Meshflow and Optical Flow from Event Cameras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tpami.2025.3615144","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we explore the problem of event-based meshflow estimation, a novel task that involves predicting a spatially smooth sparse motion field from event cameras. To start, we review the state-of-the-art in event-based flow estimation, highlighting two key areas for further research: i) the lack of meshflow-specific event datasets and methods, and ii) the underexplored challenge of event data density. First, we generate a large-scale High-Resolution Event Meshflow (HREM) dataset, which showcases its superiority by encompassing the merits of high resolution at 1280x720, handling dynamic objects and complex motion patterns, and offering both optical flow and meshflow labels. These aspects have not been fully explored in previous works. Besides, we propose Efficient Event-based MeshFlow (EEMFlow) network, a lightweight model featuring a specially crafted encoder-decoder architecture to facilitate swift and accurate meshflow estimation. Furthermore, we upgrade EEMFlow network to support dense event optical flow, in which a Confidence-induced Detail Completion (CDC) module is proposed to preserve sharp motion boundaries. We conduct comprehensive experiments to show the exceptional performance and runtime efficiency (30x faster) of our EEMFlow model compared to the recent state-of-the-art flow method. As an extension, we expand HREM into HREM+, a multi-density event dataset contributing to a thorough study of the robustness of existing methods across data with varying densities, and propose an Adaptive Density Module (ADM) to adjust the density of input event data to a more optimal range, enhancing the model's generalization ability. We empirically demonstrate that ADM helps to significantly improve the performance of EEMFlow and EEMFlow+ by 8% and 10%, respectively. Code and dataset are released at https://github.com/boomluo02/EEMFlowPlus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by TPAMI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommy","familyName":"Mordo","name":"Mordo, Tommy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagie","familyName":"Dekel","name":"Dekel, Sagie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Madmon","name":"Madmon, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moshe","familyName":"Tennenholtz","name":"Tennenholtz, Moshe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Kurland","name":"Kurland, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLRF: Competitive Search Agent Design via Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:46:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Competitive search is a setting where document publishers modify them to improve their ranking in response to a query. Recently, publishers have increasingly leveraged LLMs to generate and modify competitive content. We introduce Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback (RLRF), a framework that trains LLMs using preference datasets derived from ranking competitions. The goal of a publisher (LLM-based) agent is to optimize content for improved ranking while accounting for the strategies of competing agents. We generate the datasets using approaches that do not rely on human-authored data. We show that our proposed agents consistently and substantially outperform previously suggested approaches for LLM-based competitive document modification. We further show that our agents are effective with ranking functions they were not trained for (i.e., out of distribution) and they adapt to strategic opponents. These findings provide support to the significant potential of using reinforcement learning in competitive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Gabdullin","name":"Gabdullin, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using predefined vector systems as latent space configuration for neural network supervised training on data with arbitrarily large number of classes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:28:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised learning (SL) methods are indispensable for neural network (NN) training used to perform classification tasks. While resulting in very high accuracy, SL training often requires making NN parameter number dependent on the number of classes, limiting their applicability when the number of classes is extremely large or unknown in advance. In this paper we propose a methodology that allows one to train the same NN architecture regardless of the number of classes. This is achieved by using predefined vector systems as the target latent space configuration (LSC) during NN training. We discuss the desired properties of target configurations and choose randomly perturbed vectors of An root system for our experiments. These vectors are used to successfully train encoders and visual transformers (ViT) on Cinic-10 and ImageNet-1K in low- and high-dimensional cases by matching NN predictions with the predefined vectors. Finally, ViT is trained on a dataset with 1.28 million classes illustrating the applicability of the method to training on datasets with extremely large number of classes. In addition, potential applications of LSC in lifelong learning and NN distillation are discussed illustrating versatility of the proposed methodology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 12 figures, 10 tables, 12 equations, 1 algorithm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Qizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoshi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhuoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Code-Assisted Chain-of-Thoughts and Instructions for Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:59:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning capability is pivotal for Large Language Models (LLMs) to solve complex tasks, yet achieving reliable and scalable reasoning remains challenging. While Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has become a mainstream approach, existing methods often suffer from uncontrolled generation, insufficient quality, and limited diversity in reasoning paths. Recent efforts leverage code to enhance CoT by grounding reasoning in executable steps, but such methods are typically constrained to predefined mathematical problems, hindering scalability and generalizability. In this work, we propose Caco (Code-Assisted Chain-of-ThOught), a novel framework that automates the synthesis of high-quality, verifiable, and diverse instruction-CoT reasoning data through code-driven augmentation. Unlike prior work, Caco first fine-tunes a code-based CoT generator on existing math and programming solutions in a unified code format, then scales the data generation to a large amount of diverse reasoning traces. Crucially, we introduce automated validation via code execution and rule-based filtering to ensure logical correctness and structural diversity, followed by reverse-engineering filtered outputs into natural language instructions and language CoTs to enrich task adaptability. This closed-loop process enables fully automated, scalable synthesis of reasoning data with guaranteed executability. Experiments on our created Caco-1.3M dataset demonstrate that Caco-trained models achieve strong competitive performance on mathematical reasoning benchmarks, outperforming existing strong baselines. Further analysis reveals that Caco's code-anchored verification and instruction diversity contribute to superior generalization across unseen tasks. Our work establishes a paradigm for building self-sustaining, trustworthy reasoning systems without human intervention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Vahidi-Moghaddam","name":"Vahidi-Moghaddam, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayed Pedram Haeri","familyName":"Boroujeni","name":"Boroujeni, Sayed Pedram Haeri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloufar","familyName":"Mehrabi","name":"Mehrabi, Niloufar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:47:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the main challenges in modern control applications, particularly in robot and vehicle motion control, is achieving accurate, fast, and safe movement. To address this, optimal control policies have been developed to enforce safety while ensuring high performance. Since basic first-principles models of real systems are often available, model-based controllers are widely used. Model predictive control (MPC) is a leading approach that optimizes performance while explicitly handling safety constraints. However, obtaining accurate models for complex systems is difficult, which motivates data-driven alternatives. ML-based MPC leverages learned models to reduce reliance on hand-crafted dynamics, while reinforcement learning (RL) can learn near-optimal policies directly from interaction data. Data-enabled predictive control (DeePC) goes further by bypassing modeling altogether, directly learning safe policies from raw input-output data. Recently, large language model (LLM) agents have also emerged, translating natural language instructions into structured formulations of optimal control problems. Despite these advances, data-driven policies face significant limitations. They often suffer from slow response times, high computational demands, and large memory needs, making them less practical for real-world systems with fast dynamics, limited onboard computing, or strict memory constraints. To address this, various technique, such as reduced-order modeling, function-approximated policy learning, and convex relaxations, have been proposed to reduce computational complexity. In this paper, we present eight such approaches and demonstrate their effectiveness across real-world applications, including robotic arms, soft robots, and vehicle motion control."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengfan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuantDemoire: Quantization with Outlier Aware for Image Demoiréing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:58:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Demoiréing aims to remove moiré artifacts that often occur in images. While recent deep learning-based methods have achieved promising results, they typically require substantial computational resources, limiting their deployment on edge devices. Model quantization offers a compelling solution. However, directly applying existing quantization methods to demoiréing models introduces severe performance degradation. The main reasons are distribution outliers and weakened representations in smooth regions. To address these issues, we propose QuantDemoire, a post-training quantization framework tailored to demoiréing. It contains two key components. **First}, we introduce an outlier-aware quantizer to reduce errors from outliers. It uses sampling-based range estimation to reduce activation outliers, and keeps a few extreme weights in FP16 with negligible cost. **Second**, we design a frequency-aware calibration strategy. It emphasizes low- and mid-frequency components during fine-tuning, which mitigates banding artifacts caused by low-bit quantization. Extensive experiments validate that our QuantDemoire achieves large reductions in parameters and computation while maintaining quality. Meanwhile, it outperforms existing quantization methods by over **4 dB** on W4A4. Code is released at: https://github.com/zhengchen1999/QuantDemoire."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code is available at: https://github.com/zhengchen1999/QuantDemoire"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaFind: Scene-Aware 3D Asset Retrieval for Coherent Metaverse Scene Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:37:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present MetaFind, a scene-aware tri-modal compositional retrieval framework designed to enhance scene generation in the metaverse by retrieving 3D assets from large-scale repositories. MetaFind addresses two core challenges: (i) inconsistent asset retrieval that overlooks spatial, semantic, and stylistic constraints, and (ii) the absence of a standardized retrieval paradigm specifically tailored for 3D asset retrieval, as existing approaches mainly rely on general-purpose 3D shape representation models. Our key innovation is a flexible retrieval mechanism that supports arbitrary combinations of text, image, and 3D modalities as queries, enhancing spatial reasoning and style consistency by jointly modeling object-level features (including appearance) and scene-level layout structures. Methodologically, MetaFind introduces a plug-and-play equivariant layout encoder ESSGNN that captures spatial relationships and object appearance features, ensuring retrieved 3D assets are contextually and stylistically coherent with the existing scene, regardless of coordinate frame transformations. The framework supports iterative scene construction by continuously adapting retrieval results to current scene updates. Empirical evaluations demonstrate the improved spatial and stylistic consistency of MetaFind in various retrieval tasks compared to baseline methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The Thirty-Ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kotaro J.","familyName":"Nishimura","name":"Nishimura, Kotaro J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuichi","familyName":"Sakumura","name":"Sakumura, Yuichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazushi","familyName":"Ikeda","name":"Ikeda, Kazushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive kernel-density approach for imbalanced binary classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:45:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Class imbalance is a common challenge in real-world binary classification tasks, often leading to predictions biased toward the majority class and reduced recognition of the minority class. This issue is particularly critical in domains such as medical diagnosis and anomaly detection, where correct classification of minority classes is essential. Conventional methods often fail to deliver satisfactory performance when the imbalance ratio is extremely severe. To address this challenge, we propose a novel approach called Kernel-density-Oriented Threshold Adjustment with Regional Optimization (KOTARO), which extends the framework of kernel density estimation (KDE) by adaptively adjusting decision boundaries according to local sample density. In KOTARO, the bandwidth of Gaussian basis functions is dynamically tuned based on the estimated density around each sample, thereby enhancing the classifier's ability to capture minority regions. We validated the effectiveness of KOTARO through experiments on both synthetic and real-world imbalanced datasets. The results demonstrated that KOTARO outperformed conventional methods, particularly under conditions of severe imbalance, highlighting its potential as a promising solution for a wide range of imbalanced classification problems"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":50,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04046+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04057+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04058+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04066+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04072+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04074+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04076+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04081+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04090+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04096+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04100+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04111+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04125+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04135+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04142+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04162+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04173+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04182+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04195+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04202+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04206+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04212+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04214+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04217+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04225+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04234+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04246+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04257+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04265+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04284+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04293+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04302+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04303+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04311+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04317+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04320+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04340+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04347+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04360+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04365+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04371+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04373+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04392+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04401+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04454+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04465+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04477+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04484+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04488+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04504%29"}}